{"step_index":84,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T06:21:10Z","content":"Here is a thorough, step-by-step breakdown of **Phase 1** so you have 100% clarity on how the system works, what you are looking at, and where your code lives.\n\n---\n\n### 🎯 The 3 Objectives of Phase 1\n1. **Environment Sanity Check**: Verify that the browser renders all styles, Tailwind CSS, and Web Audio without console errors.\n2. **Mental Model of the User Flow**: Experience the demo from the judges' perspective so you understand the emotional and technical narrative.\n3. **Codebase Orientation**: Map out `frontend/index.html` line-by-line so you know exactly which DOM elements and JavaScript functions belong to you (FE 1) vs. FE 2.\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ Step 1: Launch & Inspect DevTools\n\n#### 1. Open the file in your browser\nRun this in PowerShell:\n```powershell\nstart C:\\Users\\Piyush\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\aegis_os\\frontend\\index.html\n```\n\n#### 2. Open Developer Tools (`F12` or `Ctrl + Shift + I`)\nLook at two tabs immediately:\n* **Console Tab**: Check for any red errors. You should only see clean logs like:\n  `System boot: AegisOS kernel initialized.`\n* **Elements Tab**: Notice how the page is laid out using a 12-column grid:\n  * Columns 1–5 (Left): The Virtual OS Desktop (`#desktop-viewport`)\n  * Columns 6–9 (Center): The Canvas Memory Graph (`#memory-canvas`)\n  * Columns 10–12 (Right): Your Boundary Hypervisor & Telemetry (`#hypervisor-card`)\n\n---\n\n### 🎬 Step 2: The 3-Act Demo Walkthrough (What Actually Happens)\n\nClick the blue **`[RUN 3-ACT DEMO]`** button in the top right. Here is what happens behind the scenes in JavaScript:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Presenter as FE1 / Presenter\n    participant Core as Demo Orchestrator\n    participant OS as Virtual OS (FE2)\n    participant Graph as Canvas Memory (FE2)\n    participant Hyp as Hypervisor (FE1)\n\n    Presenter->>Core: Click [RUN 3-ACT DEMO]\n    Note over Core: ACT I: THE HANDS\n    Core->>OS: Move virtual cursor & type invoice\n    Core->>Graph: Pulse active memory nodes\n    Core->>Hyp: Append to Telemetry Log: \"Voucher INV-001 settled\"\n    \n    Note over Core: ACT II: SELF-LEARNING\n    Core->>OS: Relocate Submit button (Chaos Drift)\n    Core->>Graph: Detect drift & draw relocalization vector\n    Core->>Hyp: Update Status: \"DRIFT DETECTED -> HEALED\"\n\n    Note over Core: ACT III: BOUNDARIES & TRUST\n    Core->>Hyp: Inject $48,900 Malicious Voucher\n    Hyp->>Hyp: Hypervisor triggers INTERCEPT (before mouse moves!)\n    Hyp->>Hyp: Render SHA-256 Proof-of-Boundary Card & Alarm sound\n```\n\n#### What you need to observe during each act:\n* **In Act I**: Look at the telemetry stream in the right column. It logs each field as it gets filled.\n* **In Act II**: Watch the amber alert banner pop up at the bottom of the OS window and the status pill in the top header turn amber (`SELF-HEALING`).\n* **In Act III**: Watch the right panel turn **crimson red**, the audio klaxon play, and the Proof-of-Boundary card display the SHA-256 hash.\n\n---\n\n### 🗺️ Step 3: Deep Anatomy of [`frontend/index.html`](file:///C:/Users/Piyush/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/aegis_os/frontend/index.html)\n\nThe entire frontend currently lives in a single, high-performance file for zero-latency presentation. Here is your exact map of the code:\n\n#### Part A: Structure & Styles (Lines 1–65)\n* **Lines 10–55**: Custom CSS classes:\n  * `.glass-panel`: Dark translucent background for normal state.\n  * `.glass-panel-danger`: Crimson translucent background when Act III triggers.\n  * `.glow-cyan`, `.glow-crimson`: Neon box-shadow animations.\n  * `#virtual-cursor`: The SVG cursor element and its `cubic-bezier` physics.\n\n#### Part B: The HTML Layout (Lines 66–360)\n* **Lines 67–108**: **Top Navigation Header** (Owned by **FE 1**)\n  * Contains the Æ logo, the status badge (`#system-status-badge`), the audio toggle button, and the demo trigger button.\n* **Lines 111–210**: **Virtual OS Viewport (Left Column - 5 cols)** (Owned by **FE 2**)\n  * The simulated Win32 ERP form: input boxes, submit button, and table ledger.\n* **Lines 212–285**: **Episodic Memory Graph (Center Column - 4 cols)** (Owned by **FE 2**)\n  * The `<canvas id=\"memory-canvas\">` and landmark node badges.\n* **Lines 287–358**: **Boundary Hypervisor & Telemetry (Right Column - 3 cols)** (Owned by **FE 1** ⭐)\n  * `#hypervisor-card`: The safety container.\n  * `#pob-card`: The Proof-of-Boundary card showing the SHA-256 hash.\n  * `#telemetry-log`: The live scrolling audit stream.\n\n#### Part C: The JavaScript Engine (Lines 370–650)\n* **Lines 375–445**: **Audio Engine (`playSynthSound`)**\n  * Uses browser Web Audio API to create beeps, clicks, and alarms without external MP3 files.\n* **Lines 448–560**: **Canvas Graph Loop (`animateMemoryGraph`)**\n  * Handled primarily by FE 2 (particle physics, nodes, relocalization line).\n* **Lines 562–640**: **The Demo State Machine** (Shared, but owned by **FE 1** ⭐)\n  * `runFullDemo()`: Coordinates the 3 acts.\n  * `runAct1()`: Legitimate voucher flow.\n  * `runAct2()`: Drift and self-healing flow.\n  * `runAct3()`: Boundary breach and Proof-of-Boundary enforcement.\n  * `addLog(msg, type)`: Function that writes lines into the right-hand telemetry console.\n\n---\n\n### 🛡️ Step 4: Territory Rules (FE 1 vs. FE 2)\n\nTo make sure you and FE 2 never overwrite each other:\n\n| Component | Owner | Rule |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Top Header & Global State** | **FE 1** | FE 2 should never modify `#system-status-badge` or the action buttons directly. |\n| **Right Column (Hypervisor & Logs)** | **FE 1** | This is 100% your domain. You style it, add filters, and build the PoB modal. |\n| **Center Column (`<canvas>`)** | **FE 2** | Don't edit `animateMemoryGraph()` without syncing with FE 2. |\n| **Left Column (ERP Form & Cursor)** | **FE 2** | FE 2 manages the inputs, button geometry, and `#virtual-cursor` animation. |\n| **Shared Bridge: `addLog(msg, type)`** | **Both** | FE 2 calls `addLog(...)` whenever their canvas or cursor does something; your function renders it. |\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Step 5: Your First Safe 2-Minute Exercise\n\nTo prove you have full control over Phase 1, make this tiny test modification right now:\n\n1. Open [`frontend/index.html`](file:///C:/Users/Piyush/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/aegis_os/frontend/index.html) in your editor.\n2. Search for the text: `DISBURSEMENT PIPELINE :: STRICT COMPLIANCE` (around Line 140).\n3. Change it to: `DISBURSEMENT PIPELINE :: ZERO-CHAT BOUNDARY ENCLAVE`.\n4. Refresh your browser (`Ctrl + F5`) and see your change live.\n\nOnce you see that change, **Phase 1 is officially complete**! You understand the environment, the narrative, and your territory.\n\n---\n\n### What to do next?\nWhen you're ready, say the word and we'll move into **Phase 2**: building the **Telemetry Filter Chips** and the **Interactive Proof-of-Boundary Inspection Modal** for your column!","thinking":"**Deeply Clarifying Phase 1 Setup**\n\nInitial setup requires focusing on three core tasks. The first step involves opening and inspecting the designated canvas file to establish a baseline understanding. Then, the demo must be run to generate a live output for immediate observation and to ensure correct environment configuration. This is followed by thoroughly studying the same canvas file to understand its architecture and key components.\n\n\n"}
